All commands accept `--root PATH` to select a repository; by default HDR walks up
from the current directory to the nearest `.hdr`, stopping at the Git repository
boundary.

| Command | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `hdr init [--harness NAME]...` | Create `.hdr/` with the two seed decisions. Refuses to overwrite. |
| `hdr install HARNESS --user`\|`--project` | Register the checker with `claude`, `codex`, `opencode` or `pi`. |
| `hdr validate` | Strictly validate every decision file and print how many decisions are current. |
| `hdr check [--json]` | Run all checks and save a run report. |
| `hdr inspect [--json]` | Run the same checks without saving a report. |
| `hdr doctor [--json]` | Like `inspect`, with repair guidance. |
| `hdr explain [--no-open]` | Write `.hdr/runs/report.html` and open it in a browser. |
| `hdr trace RUN_ID [--decision ID]` | Show a saved run with Git history for the files it used. |
| `hdr hook [--harness NAME]` | Handle one hook event from standard input. Harnesses call this; you do not. |

# Exit codes

| Code | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `0` | Every evaluated check passed. |
| `1` | At least one check failed. |
| `2` | Invalid input, unreadable evidence or an execution error. |

# Run reports

`check` and every hook write a JSON report to `.hdr/runs/`. A report holds the
findings, record and evidence hashes, the configuration files inspected, Git
`HEAD` and dirty status, and the hook event's name, session, tool name and
harness.

Tool inputs, file contents, prompts, transcripts and environment values are never
recorded. Reports can still contain paths, decision titles and error text, so
review them before sharing.

`trace` reads history at the `HEAD` recorded in the run, not today's `HEAD`. Git
history shows correlation, not proof of who authored a change.

# Hook input

`hdr hook` expects one JSON object of at most 1 MiB with at least
`hook_event_name`, `session_id` and `cwd`. Supported events are `SessionStart`,
`PreToolUse`, `PostToolUse` and `Stop`. Responses follow Claude Code's hook
format; the opencode and pi plugins translate them.
